In my experience orcs are popcorn monsters.  If Sting's powers worked 
against insects it might be worth something.

> If you can get to
> Expert in daggers Sting is easily worth it in the early game so you can
> survive to get a better weapon.  (Too much long term thinking can mean
> you won't ever get to the long term.)

You can get to expert in daggers just as easily without Sting.  Nearly 
all of your combat will be against non-orcs, where Sting is the same as a 
normal eleven dagger.

>   At 6.0 base damage elven broadswords are almost the best one-handed
> weapons in the game.  (Only the katana can match it due to better damage
> vs large monsters.)  Elven broadswords are far more likely to turn up
> early on. 

Elven broadswords are excellent early weapons, and very easily obtained.  
But they do have some minor flaws:
- Most classes can't gain any skill with them.
- No class can get to Expert.  In the long run, this usually doesn't 
matter because you'll #twoweapon.
- Significantly worse than long swords vs large monsters.  Again, this 
isn't an early-game consideration.

However, an ordinary elven broadsword does just fine against orcs.  
There's no reason to turn it into Orcrist.


>   For the late game, players ascend with Stormbringer.  

My last barbarian ascension was with twin +7 elven broadswords.  The one 
before it was with twin +0 elven broadswords.  Weapons aren't important 
in the late game; so long as you have good AC (conflict is very helpful 
also), nearly every monster can be walked past. The rest can be defeated 
with special methods:

Lh; - genocide
Jubi - wand of digging
Asmo, Baal - $
Orcus - Try to find a rubber chicken.  Otherwise, twin +0 silver sabers.
Vlad - Anything works, since he can't hurt you. 
Rodney - rubber chicken from his zoo or wand of death from Orcus
HPOM: wand of death


